## 3. Searching Text (you'll use this constantly)

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `grep "word" file.txt` | Find lines containing "word" |
| `grep -i "word" file.txt` | Case-insensitive search |
| `grep -r "word" ./` | Recursively search all files in a directory |
| `grep -v "word" file.txt` | Show lines that **don't** match |
| `grep -c "word" file.txt` | Count matching lines |
| `grep -E "fail|error" log.txt` | Search for multiple patterns (extended regex) |
| `cat log.txt \| grep "failed login"` | Pipe output of one command into grep |
| `awk '{print $1}' file.txt` | Print the 1st column of each line |
| `sed 's/old/new/g' file.txt` | Find & replace text |
| `sort file.txt` | Sort lines alphabetically |
| `sort -n file.txt` | Sort numerically |
| `uniq` | Remove duplicate adjacent lines (often used with `sort \| uniq`) |
| `sort file.txt \| uniq -c \| sort -nr` | Count & rank frequency of lines (e.g. most common IP in a log) |

